---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How We Verify — SEEN IN KOREA"
  description="How SEEN IN KOREA checks evidence, reviews, claims and real-world fit before making recommendations about Korea."
>
  <main>

    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="listing-hero">
      <div class="wrap">

        <div class="eyebrow">
          KOREA, VERIFIED.
        </div>

        <h1>HOW WE VERIFY</h1>

        <p>
          We do not trust ratings, popularity or marketing claims at face value.
          We check the evidence behind them — and tell you what we could not verify.
        </p>

      </div>
    </section>


    <!-- BODY -->
    <section>

      <div class="article-wrap article-body">

        <h2>What “verified” means</h2>

        <p>
          SEEN IN KOREA helps readers make better decisions about Korea —
          what to learn, where to go, what to buy and which information to trust.
        </p>

        <p>
          Verification does not mean that every question has one objectively
          correct answer. It means that our conclusions are tied to evidence,
          the limits of that evidence are made clear, and recommendations are
          based on who something actually fits.
        </p>


        <h2>We start with evidence, not rankings</h2>

        <p>
          Popularity alone does not determine our recommendations.
          Before deciding whether something is worth recommending, we look for
          evidence that can support the claims being made.
        </p>

        <p>
          Depending on the subject, that may include official information,
          government data, current prices, terms and conditions, direct testing,
          first-hand reporting, independent user experiences, interviews and
          other relevant sources.
        </p>


        <h2>What we check</h2>

        <p>
          Our verification process may examine:
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Official information</strong><br />
          Current prices, opening hours, specifications, policies, curriculum,
          eligibility requirements and other factual claims.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Independent evidence</strong><br />
          Experiences and reporting that are not controlled by the company,
          product or service being evaluated.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Review patterns</strong><br />
          We look beyond average ratings and examine recurring praise,
          recurring complaints, timing, specificity and unusual patterns.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Contradictions</strong><br />
          We compare marketing claims with independent evidence and actively
          look for information that could challenge our initial conclusion.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Real-world fit</strong><br />
          Something can be good without being right for everyone.
          We ask who it is best for — and who may be better off choosing
          something else.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Freshness</strong><br />
          Prices, policies, products and services change.
          Where relevant, we show when information was last checked.
        </p>


        <h2>Not all sources are equal</h2>

        <p>
          We generally give more weight to primary and directly verifiable
          evidence than to anonymous, promotional or repeatedly copied claims.
        </p>

        <p>
          Official documents, government information, direct testing,
          first-hand reporting and original interviews can provide stronger
          evidence for factual claims.
        </p>

        <p>
          Blogs, social posts, influencer content and general online reviews
          may still be useful, but they are considered in context rather than
          treated as proof on their own.
        </p>


        <h2>We verify claims individually</h2>

        <p>
          We do not automatically label an entire product, course, destination
          or service as verified simply because some information about it is
          confirmed.
        </p>

        <p>
          Different claims may have different levels of evidence.
          For example, we may be able to confirm a course curriculum but have
          limited evidence about long-term learning outcomes.
        </p>


        <h2>We look for evidence against our conclusion</h2>

        <p>
          A useful recommendation should survive more than supportive reviews.
          During deeper checks, we intentionally look for contradictory evidence,
          recurring complaints and realistic alternatives.
        </p>

        <p>
          When important evidence does not add up, we may highlight the
          contradiction instead of forcing a positive or negative conclusion.
        </p>


        <h2>Verdict and confidence are different</h2>

        <p>
          When SEEN IN KOREA publishes a recommendation or Navigator result,
          the conclusion and the strength of the evidence are treated separately.
        </p>

        <p>
          A recommendation may fit a particular user while the available
          evidence is still limited. In those cases, confidence should be
          shown accordingly.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where used, confidence is expressed simply as:
          <strong> High, Medium or Low.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          We avoid artificial precision such as arbitrary 92/100 scores when
          the underlying evidence cannot support that level of certainty.
        </p>


        <h2>Sometimes the answer is “More Evidence Needed”</h2>

        <p>
          We do not force a recommendation when the available evidence is too
          limited, outdated, contradictory or difficult to verify.
        </p>

        <p>
          In those cases, the most accurate conclusion may simply be:
          <strong> More Evidence Needed.</strong>
        </p>


        <h2>Best for whom?</h2>

        <p>
          SEEN IN KOREA is moving away from generic “best” lists.
          A highly rated option may still be wrong for a particular person.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our Navigator-style recommendations therefore focus on questions such as:
          what is your goal, level, budget, available time and real-life situation?
        </p>

        <p>
          Where relevant, we show:
          <strong> Best For</strong> and <strong>Skip If</strong>
          so readers can see the trade-off instead of only seeing a ranking.
        </p>


        <h2>Reviews are evidence — not the verdict</h2>

        <p>
          Online reviews can reveal useful patterns, but they can also be
          incomplete, outdated, incentivized or difficult to authenticate.
        </p>

        <p>
          We do not call individual reviews “fake” without evidence.
          If we observe unusual patterns — such as highly repetitive language,
          sudden concentrations of reviews or unclear sponsorship —
          we may reduce the weight given to that evidence and explain why.
        </p>


        <h2>Different decisions require different levels of checking</h2>

        <p>
          A restaurant opening hour does not require the same level of research
          as an expensive course, housing decision or medical service.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our research depth therefore depends on the cost, time commitment,
          reversibility and potential impact of the decision.
        </p>

        <p>
          For higher-stakes areas such as medical treatment, legal matters,
          visas, housing contracts or financial decisions, SEEN IN KOREA
          focuses on verified information, options and questions to ask rather
          than presenting consumer-style “best” verdicts.
        </p>


        <h2>What we could not verify matters too</h2>

        <p>
          Missing evidence is part of the result.
        </p>

        <p>
          When an important claim cannot be independently verified,
          we should identify that limitation rather than quietly treating the
          claim as established fact.
        </p>


        <h2>Information changes</h2>

        <p>
          Korea changes quickly. Prices, policies, availability, products and
          services may change after publication.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where appropriate, SEEN IN KOREA records when information was last
          checked and updates conclusions when material new evidence becomes
          available.
        </p>

        <p>
          If our conclusion changes, significant changes should be explained
          rather than silently rewritten.
        </p>


        <h2>Commercial relationships do not determine the verdict</h2>

        <p>
          SEEN IN KOREA may earn revenue through advertising, affiliate links,
          partnerships or other commercial activities.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our intended order is:
          <strong> research → evidence → conclusion → publication → commercial link.</strong>
        </p>

        <p>
          A product or service should not receive a more favorable editorial
          conclusion because it offers a commission or commercial relationship.
          Relevant commercial relationships are disclosed where appropriate.
        </p>


        <h2>Businesses can respond</h2>

        <p>
          A company, institution or service provider may contact SEEN IN KOREA
          if it believes information is inaccurate, outdated or missing
          important context.
        </p>

        <p>
          A provider response may be added as relevant context, but a company
          statement is not automatically treated as independent verification.
        </p>


        <h2>Readers can challenge our findings</h2>

        <p>
          Readers with recent first-hand experience can help us identify
          outdated information, missing evidence or experiences that differ
          from the patterns we found.
        </p>

        <p>
          New information is reviewed as evidence.
          One individual report does not automatically overturn an existing
          conclusion, but meaningful new evidence can trigger a re-check.
        </p>


        <h2>Our methodology will evolve</h2>

        <p>
          SEEN IN KOREA is building this verification system progressively.
          Not every article or recommendation currently uses the same depth
          of research.
        </p>

        <p>
          As we collect more original interviews, direct testing and
          user-reported outcomes, our goal is to rely increasingly on
          first-party evidence rather than simply repeating information
          available elsewhere.
        </p>


        <h2>Editorial standards</h2>

        <p>
          This page explains how our recommendation and verification process works.
          For our broader rules on sourcing, corrections, interviews,
          artificial intelligence and editorial independence, read our
          <a href="/editorial-standards">Editorial Standards →</a>
        </p>


        <h2>Corrections and evidence</h2>

        <p>
          To report outdated information, provide additional evidence or
          raise an editorial concern:
          <a href="mailto:seeninkorea@gmail.com">seeninkorea@gmail.com</a>
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
